What is Urea?
Urea (CO(NH₂)₂) is a nitrogen-rich organic compound synthesized from ammonia and carbon dioxide under high pressure. As the most concentrated solid nitrogen fertilizer (containing 46% nitrogen), it's fundamental to modern agriculture. Beyond crop nutrition, urea serves as a critical industrial feedstock for:
•	Melamine production for plastics
•	Urea-formaldehyde resins in wood adhesives
•	Diesel exhaust fluid (DEF/AdBlue) for emissions control
•	Pharmaceutical intermediates
